swer/id #uuid "115c6f60-0827-5c56-88bb-33a7c75fe9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "115c6f60-0827-5c56-88bb-33a7c75fe9ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701827} :answer-field/id #uuid "f52d9840-b109-578f-a543-2d233cab9ee8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701848}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a33a59bd-06b0-5352-a835-95dd2ae4321d" :question/problem "Complete the square on $x^2-11x+5$.\n\nHalve the linear coefficient, then add and subtract the square of that half. Fill in the nonnegative numbers.\n\n$x^2-11x+5=(x-$ {{field-1}} $)^2-$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{11}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^2-11x+5&=\\underbrace{x^2-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+\\frac{121}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{121}{4}+5 \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{11}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{121}{4}+5 \\\\\n&=\\left(x-\\frac{11}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{101}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-11x$.\n\nThe blanks are $\\frac{11}{2}$ and $\\frac{101}{4}$; the sign before the second blank is already supplied."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ebd5ce0-3374-5d08-8bb5-4f177c67b3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ebd5ce0-3374-5d08-8bb5-4f177c67b3d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701833} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d8fe81b-50fe-57b6-86a3-3883f35e02a9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701835} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a27af993-b969-5758-baca-64acf1cf0b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27af993-b969-5758-baca-64acf1cf0b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701838} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc7a11da-3e23-5400-a88a-98bfa33e2a5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186701842}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c48fd552-4bbe-5cdc-bdc9-a25757cba3fd" :question/problem "Complete the square on $z^2-9z+\\frac{83}{4}$.\n\nHalve the linear coefficient, then add and subtract the square of that half. Fill in the nonnegative numbers.\n\n$z^2-9z+\\frac{83}{4}=(z-$ {{field-1}} $)^2+$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{9}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^2-9z+\\frac{83}{4}&=\\underbrace{z^2-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}z+\\frac{81}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{83}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(z-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{83}{4} \\\\\n&=\\left(z-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)^2+\\frac{1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-9z$.\n\nThe blanks are $\\frac{9}{2}$ and $\\frac{1}{2}$; the sign before the second blank is already supplied."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc557750-8348-583d-bd3a-b0b76cb3024d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{161}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc557750-8348-583d-bd3a-b0b76cb3024d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{161}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705090} :answer-field/id #uuid "21722a7f-be11-5e00-a495-245bb67111df" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce0a2689-809f-534b-bd7d-6f06edde4c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce0a2689-809f-534b-bd7d-6f06edde4c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705089} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bfcb892-42e5-50f4-b5df-27c8154ebe31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705079}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4af91f21-e1a4-510f-8d7f-942bb3a24b1b" :question/problem "Rewrite $2-13y+y^2$ in the form $(y+a)^2+b$. Find the signed numbers $a$ and $b$.\n\n$a=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$b=$ {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Put the quadratic term first: $2-13y+y^2=y^2-13y+2$.\n\nThe half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{13}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^2-13y+2&=\\underbrace{y^2-2\\cdot \\frac{13}{2}y+\\frac{169}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{169}{4}+2 \\\\\n&=\\left(y-\\frac{13}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{169}{4}+2 \\\\\n&=\\left(y-\\frac{13}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{161}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-13y$.\n\nComparing with $(y+a)^2+b$ gives $a=-\\frac{13}{2}$ and $b=-\\frac{161}{4}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f957588-c9f2-50c4-83ea-0141d27a9eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f957588-c9f2-50c4-83ea-0141d27a9eb6" :answer/type